from pathlib import Path
import re,json,csv,hashlib,statistics
P=Path('paper/sac2027/v15_feedback'); B=Path('reports/sac2027_patch_v14')
s=(P/'revision/base.md').read_text(); manifest=json.loads((P/'revision/base.md.block-manifest.json').read_text())
parts=list(re.finditer(r'<!--block:(B\d+)-->\n(.*?)(?=<!--block:B\d+-->\n|\Z)',s,re.S))
blocks={m.group(1):m.group(2) for m in parts}; hashes={b['block_id']:b['old_hash'] for b in manifest['blocks']}; updates={}; ids={}
def change(old,new,item):
 found=[k for k,v in blocks.items() if old in v]
 assert len(found)==1,(item,old[:85],found)
 k=found[0]; v=updates.get(k,blocks[k]); assert v.count(old)==1
 updates[k]=v.replace(old,new); ids.setdefault(k,[]).append(item)
change('Next-Phase Elimination Risk in Battle Royale: Separating the Contributions of Inputs, Objectives, and Model Structure','Predicting Next-Phase Elimination in Battle Royale: Target Alignment, Team Context, and Model Structure','F1')
old=s.split('\\begin{abstract}\n')[1].split('\\end{abstract}')[0]
new='''We study next-phase team elimination from spectator-view PUBG telemetry through a controlled empirical comparison of established prediction methods. From 13,948 matches, we construct 817,766 team--phase samples with a common feature cutoff. Training, validation, and testing use successive patches; primary synthesis covers 11 cohorts. Reference gradient-boosted trees and PTR-Former attain mean AUCs of 0.768 and 0.769, while combining our inputs with reconstructed KRAFTON inputs raises tree AUC to 0.786. PTR improves within-phase discrimination over the reference tree, but their mean within-match AUCs are both 0.747. Equal-trial tuning in two large cohorts yields no consistent architecture winner. Truncating Cox learning to the next phase recovers most of cross-entropy's within-match advantage over full-follow-up learning; cross-entropy retains a within-phase advantage of 0.006--0.008. The survival adaptation performs better on its original winner-prediction task. Competition and remaining members retain risk associations after AI adjustment, whereas a movement residual is population-sensitive. These findings support aligning prediction horizon, inputs, and evaluation unit before attributing gains to architecture. The evidence concerns retrospective analysis with outcome-defined eligibility, not validated online deployment.
'''
change(old,new,'F1')
change("This probability can support match review, broadcasts, and, after validation, zone-rule review~\\cite{PUBGBlueZone2026}. Joining trajectories with event and environmental records permits analysis of interactions and environmental change~\\cite{Zheng15}. PUBG's public telemetry~\\cite{PUBGAPI} supports our spectator-view task, which uses all teams' positions but excludes the newly revealed zone. It does not represent a team's private information.","We study retrospective match review from spectator-view telemetry: an analyst compares teams' next-phase risks at a shared boundary. Broadcast use and zone-rule review require separate validation~\\cite{PUBGBlueZone2026}. Joining trajectories with event and environmental records supports this analysis~\\cite{Zheng15}. PUBG's public telemetry~\\cite{PUBGAPI} provides all teams' positions; our inputs exclude the newly revealed zone and differ from a team's private information.",'P1-4')
change('How much of the difference do inputs, objective, phase baseline, and model structure each explain, and does this vary by cohort?','How do inputs, objective, phase baseline, and model structure change performance under the evaluated controls, and does this vary by cohort?','P1-3')
change('We construct common-cutoff samples with patch-separated evaluation (Section~\\ref{sec:method}), distinguish stable habitat associations from population-sensitive ones (Section~\\ref{sec:results:rq1}), and compare adapted designs through conditional paths and matched controls for tuning, horizon, population, history, and graph processing (Sections~\\ref{sec:results:rq2}--\\ref{sec:results:rq3}).','First, we define the task and information cutoff and construct patch-separated samples with reconstruction checks (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). Second, we compare established designs using matched controls for inputs, horizon, mapping, and tuning, alongside their original task (Sections~\\ref{sec:results:rq2}--\\ref{sec:results:rq3}). Third, we identify complementary inputs and evaluation-dependent structural effects, supported by interpretable associations (Section~\\ref{sec:results:rq1}). The contribution is empirical guidance for this task, not a new hazard estimator.','F1')
change('The game versions in this window are PC updates (patches) 40.2--41.2~\\cite{PUBGPatchNotes2026}; apart from some map elements the safe-zone rules were unchanged, and the zone revamp~\\cite{PUBGBlueZone2026} was applied after collection.','The collection spans PC patches 40.2--41.2~\\cite{PUBGPatchNotes2026}, before the zone revamp~\\cite{PUBGBlueZone2026}. Update 41.1 changed bot behavior and positioning and introduced Jammer Pack tiers with different Blue Zone protection. A shared zone schedule therefore does not imply a stationary prediction environment.','P0-3')
change('Prior processed data are unavailable~\\cite{Sagong25a,KRAFTON}; we will release our code upon publication.','Prior processed data are unavailable~\\cite{Sagong25a,KRAFTON}; our reconstructed inputs and available artifacts are described in Section~\\ref{sec:discussion:limits}.','P1-4')
change('$\\Delta$ allows for the roughly 10\\,s position interval and interpolation, and the same cutoff applies to alive status, team relations, and the set of teams a model may attend to.','$\\Delta$ provides an observation buffer; inputs, relations, and attention keys use records strictly before $a_{m,k}$. The 10\\,s grid selects each player\'s nearest position within $\\pm5$\\,s (earlier wins a tie), admitting only grid times $t$ with $t+5<a_{m,k}$. Thus both sides of the sampling window precede the cutoff; no future endpoint is used.','P0-1')
change('The teams predicted at phase $k$ are those still alive at the prediction time, the risk set $\\mathcal{R}_{m,k}=\\{i: T_{m,i}>b_{m,k}\\}$; teams eliminated in the 10\\,s between the cutoff and the prediction time therefore drop out of that phase. Since $T_{m,i}$ uses later records, we check the risk sets against alive status from records before the cutoff. Only two of 817,766 rows lack an observed living member; both are in training, with none in validation or testing.','The retrospective risk set is $\\mathcal{R}_{m,k}=\\{i:T_{m,i}>b_{m,k}\\}$. Eligibility at $b_{m,k}$ is distinct from inputs fixed at $a_{m,k}$: teams finally eliminated in the intervening 10\\,s are excluded. Only two of 817,766 included rows lack an observed living member at the cutoff (both training; none validation/test). This check covers retained rows, not all excluded candidates, and does not establish equivalence to eligibility reconstructed using only events available at $b_{m,k}$. In particular, future returns can affect final-elimination time. Online risk-set validity remains unverified.','P0-1')
change('This is a landmark discrete-time hazard: the next-interval event probability among survivors at a reference time~\\cite{vanHouwelingen07}.','This is an interval event probability~\\cite{vanHouwelingen07}, not a per-second rate; interval lengths may differ. Events at $b_{m,k}$ are excluded from the current risk set, and events at the right endpoint enter its label. Boundaries are excluded if match end is missing or a gap exceeding 60\\,s in the pooled position stream overlaps the outcome window; this filter does not establish completeness of every player\'s log.','P0-1')
change('$\\ell$ is the horizontal path traveled during the observed phase window,','$\\ell$ sums horizontal displacements of the team centroid over alive grid points from phase start to the cutoff,','P1-1')
change('It is not time remaining or a forecast of future travel.','It is an empirical proxy, not remaining time or a future travel requirement; motion away from the zone also contributes to $\\ell$.','P1-1')
change('\\emph{Local competition density} is the number of other teams within 200\\,m, and \\emph{remaining members} is the number of members judged alive at the cutoff.','\\emph{Local competition density} counts other teams once each at a 3-D centroid distance strictly below 200\\,m at the focal team\'s last alive grid point. Centroids average sampled members with positive or missing health; grid points without a living member are excluded from these distances. \\emph{Remaining members} uses the separate kill/return timeline at the cutoff.','P1-1')
change('Adding the same constant to all scores leaves this term unchanged, so the objective alone cannot identify that phase 4 is riskier overall than phase 2, i.e., $\\alpha_k$ in \\eqref{eq:logit}.','Both the eliminated-team term and survivor-complement terms depend on these normalized scores; the time-gap weights depend on outcomes, not on a common score offset. Thus the full weighted objective is shift-invariant and does not identify an absolute phase risk level. Before phase-specific mapping, the reference adaptation uses validation-fitted Platt scaling $p=\\sigma(A s+B)$, where $\\sigma(u)=(1+e^{-u})^{-1}$.','P0-2')
change('Five comparisons were pre-specified:','Five primary comparisons are inherited from the earlier analysis:','P1-3')
change('Because all teams\' risks rise in later phases, predicting the phase alone can raise AUC,','Because elimination rates vary by phase, predicting the phase alone can raise AUC,','P1-2')
change('Probability accuracy uses log loss and Brier score.','Within-phase AUC can compare a phase-4 team in one match with a phase-4 team in another; within-match AUC only compares teams in the same match and phase. Probability accuracy uses log loss and Brier score.','P1-2')
change('Following pre-specified rules,','Following the inherited rules,','P1-3')
change('Holm correction applies to the five pre-specified comparisons~\\cite{Holm79}; the rest are exploratory. The five comparisons and SESOI are inherited from the earlier dated study protocol. The patch split, eligibility rule, and initial additional controls were specified before the revised runs but after earlier results had been examined; they are not a prospectively preregistered replication.','Holm correction applies to the five one-sided superiority tests against zero~\\cite{Holm79}; the 90\\% equivalence intervals are unadjusted. A ``gain'' combines an estimate above the SESOI with evidence against zero, not evidence that the true effect exceeds the SESOI. Table~\\ref{tab:primary} reports the full decisions. These are inherited analysis rules, not independently time-stamped preregistration. The patch protocol was recorded before revised training but after earlier results were examined. Bootstrap intervals condition on fitted models; the cohorts share a collection window and are not independent external replications.','P1-3')
change('Our reference design attains mean AUC 0.768 (GBDT) and 0.769 (PTR-Former),','The combined-input GBDT has the highest mean next-phase AUC in the reference matrix (0.786), with log loss 0.482 and Brier score 0.157; it uses additional information, so this is not an equal-information architecture comparison. With our inputs alone, mean AUC is 0.768 (GBDT) and 0.769 (PTR-Former),','F1')
change('Of the five inherited primary comparisons, three meet practical equivalence:','\\input{tables/primary_decisions}\nOf the five inherited primary comparisons, three meet practical equivalence:','P1-3')
change('The team-axis AUC verdict would be a gain for a threshold at or below 0.00428 and equivalent for a threshold above 0.00551; intermediate thresholds are inconclusive.','Using unrounded results, the team-axis AUC rule gives gain for $0<\\delta\\leq0.00428373554$, inconclusive for $0.00428373554<\\delta<0.00550900835$, and equivalent for $\\delta\\geq0.00550900835$. The analysis retains $\\delta=0.005$; these ranges describe sensitivity, not a new threshold.','P1-3')
change('These results do not establish that graph structure is universally unhelpful.','This pattern is consistent with context helping distinguish different matches at the same phase, but does not identify an attention mechanism. It does not establish that graph structure is universally unhelpful.','P1-2')
change('The lite model includes required speed, competition density, log alive teams, and remaining members, with phase intercepts.','The lite model uses required speed, competition density, log alive teams, remaining members, and phase intercepts. Its mean overall/within-phase AUCs are 0.719/0.677, versus 0.615/0.500 for phase-only and 0.768/0.742 for full GBDT. Mean log loss/Brier are 0.532/0.178 for lite and 0.575/0.194 for phase-only.','P0-2')
change('Next-phase Cox recovers essentially the same within-match performance as BCE under the tested recipe, identifying horizon alignment as a major contributor.','Next-phase Cox narrows the within-match gap to BCE under the tested recipe, supporting horizon alignment as a major contributor; intervals containing zero alone do not prove equivalence.','P1-3')
change('Phase-specific probabilities can support match review and broadcasts, or zone-rule comparisons after validation under new rules; live use requires real-time feeds. Practical evaluation should verify a common cutoff by rebuilding inputs, report within-phase and within-match discrimination alongside overall AUC and calibration, and align the estimand and information set before comparing architectures.','The supported application is retrospective spectator-view match review. We have not measured analyst benefit, event retrieval at a chosen top-$k$, or end-to-end latency. Live use additionally requires an online eligibility audit and validation of feed availability, feature computation, and inference. Phase-average calibration error alone does not establish calibration within risk strata.','P1-4')
change('Five limits bound these findings. First,','Further limits bound these findings. First,','P1-4')
change('ten weeks and three patches under one zone rule,','ten weeks and three patches,','P0-3')
change('Equal trials do not equalize capacity or computation; bootstrap intervals condition on those seeds.','Equal trials do not equalize capacity or computation; bootstrap intervals condition on those seeds. Full-feature regularized logistic and standalone MLP baselines, all-cohort multi-seed tuning, and normal/ranked-stratified effects remain untested. Recorded runtimes across devices or concurrent jobs are not a controlled efficiency benchmark.\n\n\\paragraph{Artifacts and availability.}\nThe manuscript source package rebuilds the tables and figures from included source assets; it does not reproduce model training. We retain the split manifest, protocol, feature definitions, configurations, environment records, and prediction hashes locally. Public anonymous access to the full experiment artifacts and raw telemetry is not established, so we do not describe this as a released benchmark.','P1-4')
change('Structural effects depend on metric and threshold, and input sets remain complementary.','Combining the two input sets gives the highest mean next-phase AUC in the reference matrix; equal-trial comparisons yield no consistent architecture winner. Structural effects depend on metric and threshold.','F1')
def compact(prefix,new,item='F2'):
 found=[k for k in blocks if prefix in updates.get(k,blocks[k])]
 assert len(found)==1,(prefix,found)
 k=found[0]; old=updates.get(k,blocks[k]); tail='\n\n' if old.endswith('\n\n') else '\n'
 updates[k]=old[:old.index(prefix)]+new+tail;ids.setdefault(k,[]).append(item)
compact('Existing PUBG designs address',r"Sagong et al.\ classify individual survival from activity scores~\cite{Sagong25a} or predict wins from strategy sequences~\cite{Sagong25b}. KRAFTON~\cite{KRAFTON} learns elimination-order risk with cross-team attention and converts it to win probability. These targets differ from next-phase elimination probability. Inputs, prediction units, and metrics also differ, so a performance gap alone does not identify the design choice responsible.")
compact('We formulate next-phase risk',r"We compare these designs on a common landmark discrete-time task. A habitat-loss view~\cite{Fahrig03} organizes boundary exposure, movement~\cite{Nathan08}, competition, and members; it guides covariates rather than asserting an ecological mechanism.")
compact('These data had been analyzed',r"These matches appeared in earlier analyses: the split is a retrospective temporal reevaluation, not an untouched prospective test. Selection uses patch 41.1 and evaluation uses 41.2. Of 145,019 primary test rows, 19,728 (13.6\%) are AI teams; elimination rates are 37.9\% for AI and 25.1\% for human teams, motivating human-only evaluation.")
compact('The baselines are adaptations',r"These are task adaptations (Table~\ref{tab:compare}). KRAFTON uses commit 99ed9d1 with fixes to score direction, padding, squad truncation, and 55/57-dimensional inference. Its 55 features include 32 definition-matched reconstructions, 22 proxies, and one zero-filled feature, plus two zone-distance ratios. Sagong is a paper-based reimplementation with attack/assist proxies and team/full-phase aggregation of its solo/half-phase task. Neither reproduces original empirical performance. We separately report winner accuracy and elimination-order C-index; the upstream `IBS' is snapshot winner Brier, not next-phase Brier.")
compact('The reference GBDT uses',r"Reference GBDT uses 200 trees and learning rate 0.06. PTR uses width 128, four heads, two blocks, dropout 0.1, and AdamW at $10^{-3}$ with cosine decay, at most 12 epochs, and validation-NLL stopping. KRAFTON uses width 64, eight heads/layers, batch 512, AdamW at $10^{-4}$, and validation-loss stopping; its epochs sample many time points.")
compact('The unadjusted movement residual',r"The movement coefficient falls from $+0.069$ $[0.052,\allowbreak 0.086]$ without AI adjustment to $+0.005$ $[-0.007,\allowbreak 0.018]$ with adjustment and $+0.010$ $[-0.004,\allowbreak 0.023]$ in human teams. Its interval excludes zero at 500\,m competition radius, but not 100/200\,m. Competition and member directions survive these checks. Median negative-residual fractions across cohorts are 88.1\% for human and 93.8\% for AI teams; $d>0$ occurs in 37.4\%/28.2\%, with median observed speeds 2.35/2.25\,m/s. Thus it rarely describes an unmet positive travel requirement. Separating required and observed speeds gives positive coefficients ($+0.395$/$+0.025$ per SD), but raises maximum VIF to 12.55 and identifies neither directional progress nor causality.")
compact('Figure~\\ref{fig:rq1} uses',r"In within-band SD units (Fig.~\ref{fig:rq1}), inside depth is near zero early, then $-0.224$/$-0.414$ middle/late (11/11). Competition is $+0.385$/$+0.213$/$+0.237$, and members $-0.516$/$-0.601$/$-0.617$. Equality tests reject constant depth and competition slopes on common-SD ($p=1.6\times10^{-6}$/$7.7\times10^{-8}$) and band-SD scales ($4.1\times10^{-7}$/$2.4\times10^{-6}$). Model-implied $+1$ enemy-team changes are 9.8/3.7/3.9\,pp; $+1$ member changes are $-6.6$/$-10.5$/$-13.3$\,pp. These are observational discrete changes, not intervention effects; the latter can exceed roster capacity.")
compact('On the KRAFTON path, aligning',r"The KRAFTON path adds 0.0105 mean AUC through input/row alignment, 0.0209 through the phase baseline, 0.0156 through the BCE recipe, $-0.0003$ through explicit intercepts, 0.0047 through team-axis PTR, and $-0.0020$ through phase/relation processing. Unrounded increments sum to the endpoint difference. Validation rather than training fit changes baseline AUC from 0.7511 to 0.7513. These order-dependent recipe changes bundle horizon, optimization, and mapping choices; matched controls follow.")
compact('\\paragraph{Structural effects depend',r"\paragraph{Structural effects depend on metric and threshold.}\n\input{tables/primary_decisions}\nThree primary comparisons meet practical equivalence; both team-axis comparisons are inconclusive (Table~\ref{tab:primary}). REML/Hartung--Knapp retains these verdicts. Team-axis AUC is positive (Holm $p<0.001$), but its 90\% interval crosses 0.005; the rank-correlation interval crosses 0.01. For AUC, unrounded thresholds give gain at $0<\delta\leq0.00428373554$, inconclusive at $0.00428373554<\delta<0.00550900835$, and equivalent at $\delta\geq0.00550900835$. We retain $\delta=0.005$.")
compact('The winner/order task favors',r"The survival recipe better predicts winners: KRAFTON exceeds PTR by $0.0175$ $[0.0020,\allowbreak 0.0324]$, while their C-index difference is uncertain ($0.0024$ $[-0.0005,\allowbreak 0.0053]$). With our inputs/backbone fixed, weighted survival exceeds BCE by $0.0252$ winner accuracy and $0.0065$ C-index (both intervals exclude zero). These use equal-cohort means and paired match resampling.")
compact('\\paragraph{Population restrictions',r"\paragraph{Population restrictions have model-dependent effects.}\nRemoving AI input changes human AUC by $-0.0012$ to $+0.0010$ across six model--cohort pairs. Human-only loss raises PTR human AUC by $+0.0018$ $[0.0009,\allowbreak 0.0028]$ in Erangel and $+0.0016$ $[0.0010,\allowbreak 0.0022]$ in Taego, but lowers KRAFTON's Erangel AUC by 0.0022. Other KRAFTON/GBDT changes are uncertain. Opponents remain context.")
compact('\\paragraph{Additional history',r"\paragraph{Additional history has limited incremental value.}\nPast-token summaries lower KRAFTON AUC by 0.0033/0.0028 in Erangel/Taego (both intervals exclude zero). Ordered versus shuffled PTR prefixes yield $+0.0001$ $[-0.0001,\allowbreak 0.0003]$ and $+0.0010$ $[0.0006,\allowbreak 0.0013]$. Current tokens retain cumulative summaries and phase covariates; this does not establish that history is uninformative.")
compact('Habitat context closes more',r"Removing habitat inputs worsens prediction. KRAFTON inputs outperform ours within matches under GBDT; their union improves further, indicating complementary information.")
compact('Team attention improves within-phase AUC, while',r"Team attention improves within-phase AUC but remains inconclusive on primary overall-AUC/rank-correlation thresholds. GATv2 detects small alignment effects and scores close to KRAFTON. Existing relation summaries may limit the additional value of explicit graphs.")
# Convert explicit line escapes in compact paragraph boundaries, not LaTeX commands.
for k in updates:updates[k]=updates[k].replace(r'}\n\input', '}\n\\input').replace(r'}\nThree','}\nThree').replace(r'}\nRemoving','}\nRemoving').replace(r'}\nPast-token','}\nPast-token')

compact('\\paragraph{Contributions.}',r"\paragraph{Contributions.}\nWe define common-cutoff, patch-separated samples with reconstruction checks (Section~\ref{sec:method}); compare established designs through matched controls and their original task (Sections~\ref{sec:results:rq2}--\ref{sec:results:rq3}); and identify complementary inputs, metric-dependent structural effects, and interpretable associations (Section~\ref{sec:results:rq1}). This is an empirical study, not a new hazard estimator.")
compact('We add GATv2 with edge',r"Exploratory GATv2~\cite{Brody22} uses the official PyG 2.7.0 operator, four continuous relation channels, BCE, and the same 16 configurations/three seeds. Training graphs are nearly complete (99.4\%/99.5\% mean density), motivating direct use of edge features rather than shortest-path distinctions. Each match--phase is a separate graph, with no new pruning or temporal module. A fixed-configuration control jointly relabels relation channels. Neural references are retrained on CPU at their original selected settings. This extension was specified after some original results.")
compact('To address unequal tuning',r"Equal-trial tuning~\cite{Bergstra12,Cawley10} compares GBDT, a KRAFTON-derived team-only backbone, and PTR without ranking loss in the two largest cohorts. Each uses the same 153 columns (150 features, centroid coordinates, AI indicator), BCE, and 16 configurations selected by validation NLL. Neural models share the search list, 12-epoch cap, three-epoch patience, and eight-match batches. Selected settings use seeds 42/13/2026; metrics average runs, not ensembles, and intervals condition on these seeds. Equal trials do not equalize computation; parameters/runtimes are recorded. Reference GBDT uses 151 columns; original-input adaptations omit the AI indicator.")
compact('Further limits bound these findings.',r"Further limits are spectator-view rather than private inputs; ten weeks, three patches, and one solo cohort; no untouched prospective or new-rule test; and Karakin's six training matches. Baselines use proxy inputs without original checkpoints and change Sagong's unit/interval. Habitat associations are observational: teams choose positions, and zone/combat damage are not separated. Tuning covers two cohorts, a limited search, and three seeds, without equalizing capacity/computation. Full-feature logistic/standalone MLP baselines, all-cohort multi-seed tuning, and normal/ranked-stratified effects remain untested. Mixed-device/concurrent runtimes are not an efficiency benchmark.")
compact('Competition and remaining members retain their directions across robustness checks.',r"Competition and members retain their directions; competition is strongest early and inside depth/members later. The depth pattern resembles an ecological edge effect~\cite{Ries04} without identifying its mechanism. Population-sensitive movement and the lite model's 73\% retained within-phase discrimination support cautious interpretation, not causality.")
k=next(k for k in updates if 'Further limits are spectator-view' in updates[k]); updates[k]+=r'''\paragraph{Artifacts and availability.}
The source package recompiles the manuscript; it does not reproduce training. Local artifacts retain split/protocol hashes, feature definitions, configurations, environment records, and prediction hashes. Public anonymous access to experiment artifacts/raw telemetry is not established; this is not a released benchmark.
'''
